('NCLEX_SATA_511055', 'SATA',
 'A client is admitted to the recovery area immediately after an emergency caesarean birth performed under spinal anaesthesia. Which assessments are priorities during the first hour? Select all that apply.',
 'Recovery after caesarean birth combines postoperative and postnatal priorities. Airway and breathing come first, because a spinal block that rises too high can impair the respiratory muscles, so respiratory rate, oxygen saturation and the level of the sensory block are checked repeatedly. Postnatal haemorrhage risk is unchanged by the surgical route, so fundal tone, lochia and the wound dressing are assessed, along with urine output through the catheter and the return of lower limb sensation and movement before any attempt to mobilise.',
 '{"options":[{"id":"A","text":"Respiratory rate, oxygen saturation and the level of the sensory block"},{"id":"B","text":"Fundal tone and height"},{"id":"C","text":"Amount of lochia and the wound dressing for oozing"},{"id":"D","text":"Return of sensation and movement in both legs"},{"id":"E","text":"Urine output through the indwelling catheter"},{"id":"F","text":"Encouraging the client to walk unaided to the toilet"}]}'::jsonb,
 '{"answers":["A","B","C","D","E"],"feedback":{"A":"Correct. A block that rises too high impairs the respiratory muscles, so ventilation is monitored closely.","B":"Correct. Uterine atony and haemorrhage remain a risk after caesarean as after vaginal birth.","C":"Correct. Lochia and the dressing together reveal both uterine and surgical bleeding.","D":"Correct. Sensation and movement must return before the client can safely mobilise.","E":"Correct. Urine output reflects perfusion and detects bladder injury after abdominal surgery.","F":"Incorrect. The client cannot mobilise safely until the block has worn off and stability is confirmed."}}'::jsonb,
 'Physiological Integrity', 'Reduction of Risk Potential',
 'Maternity', 'Multisystem', 'Caesarean birth', 'Immediate postoperative priorities',
